From 4376290d1dd340353d2df57f4b2e17a196fbbf5e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: eileenthg <40307498+eileenthg@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 01:26:03 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 10/12] Fix static loot modifier JSON for 1.21 loot condition
 API changes

These 6 hand-written loot_modifiers/*.json files (raw_goat, cooked_goat,
scavenging_animal_fat, and the compat/ scavenging_ham/smoked_ham/acorn_block
variants) were never touched by the earlier bulk condition-JSON migration
pass, which only handled the forge:conditional recipe-wrapping format. They
still used three now-invalid pieces of old-Forge-era loot condition syntax,
causing "Could not decode GlobalLootModifier" warnings (and the modifiers
silently not applying) on every server boot:

- minecraft:random_chance_with_looting was removed; ported to the real
  1.21 minecraft:random_chance_with_enchanted_bonus (unenchanted_chance +
  a minecraft:linear enchanted_chance keyed to minecraft:looting), matching
  the field layout used by FD's own real scavenging_ham_from_pig.json.
- forge:loot_table_id -> neoforge:loot_table_id (same "loot_table_id" field).
- entity_properties' EntityTarget enum serialized names changed:
  "killer" -> "attacker", "killer_player" -> "attacking_player"; the
  equipment item-predicate field also renamed "tag" -> "items" (confirmed
  against FD's own shipped scavenging_ham_from_pig.json).

Verified via ./gradlew runServer: the "Could not decode GlobalLootModifier"
warnings for these 6 ids are gone and the server reaches "Done" cleanly.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
